  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Scientific Research has $314M in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Scientific Research won 91 awards with an average award size of $3.5M, and obligations fell 36.9% year over year. Scientific Research received the largest share of FY2026 to date obligations from the Department of Defense, which obligated $268M. Scientific Research also received federal money from the General Services Administration, the Department of State, the Department of Transportation, and the Department of Homeland Security. Engineering Services is the largest market for Scientific Research with $236M in obligations, followed by All Other Professional, Scientific, and Technical Services with $51M. Historical obligations for Scientific Research show year-by-year totals of $303M in FY21, $288M in FY22, $337M in FY23, $422M in FY24, and $498M in FY25, before the current FY2026 to date total of $314M. The FY2026 to date figures are partial and represent fiscal year to date obligations.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who are Scientific Research's biggest customers?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the biggest customer for Scientific Research, obligating $268M in FY2026 to date. The General Services Administration is the next largest buyer, obligating $41.1M. The Department of State, the Department of Transportation, and the Department of Homeland Security also bought from Scientific Research, obligating $4.9M, $673K, and $58.8K respectively."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What does Scientific Research sell to the government?",
        "body": "Engineering Services is the largest market for Scientific Research with $236M in obligations in FY2026 to date. Scientific Research also sells All Other Professional, Scientific, and Technical Services ($51M), Search, Detection, Navigation, Guidance, Aeronautical, and Nautical System and Instrument Manufacturing ($19.4M), and Other Computer Related Services ($6.6M). Scientific Research has smaller obligations in markets including Aircraft Manufacturing ($1.6M) and Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) ($500)."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much does Scientific Research make from federal contracts?",
      "a": "Scientific Research has $314M in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Scientific Research won 91 awards so far this fiscal year with an average award size of $3.5M, and obligations fell 36.9% year over year. Historical annual obligations for Scientific Research were $303M in FY21, $288M in FY22, $337M in FY23, $422M in FY24, and $498M in FY25."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which agencies buy from Scientific Research?",
      "a": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer from Scientific Research, obligating $268M in FY2026 to date. The General Services Administration obligated $41.1M. The Department of State obligated $4.9M, the Department of Transportation obligated $673K, and the Department of Homeland Security obligated $58.8K."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does Scientific Research sell to the government?",
      "a": "Scientific Research sells primarily Engineering Services and related professional work to the government. Engineering Services accounted for $236M in FY2026 to date obligations, followed by All Other Professional, Scientific, and Technical Services at $51M, Search, Detection, Navigation, Guidance, Aeronautical, and Nautical System and Instrument Manufacturing at $19.4M, and Other Computer Related Services at $6.6M. Scientific Research also has obligations in markets including Aircraft Manufacturing ($1.6M) and Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) ($500)."
    },
